I began today's session with a coding challenge on Codewars.
These were the instructions:
Given two integers a and b (which can be positive or negative), find the sum of all 
the integers between and including them.If the two numbers are equal, return a or b.

First, I checked if a and b are equal; if so, the function returns a.
Then, I ensured that the starting number is always less than the ending number using 
min() and max().
After that, I stored the total number of terms in a variable called n and returned the sum 
of the numbers using the Gauss summation formula.

Even though my formula already handles the edge case where a and b are equal, I still 
implemented an if statement to make the code more readable and understandable for others. 
This also improves performance slightly by avoiding unnecessary calculations.

Then I did a coding challenge on freeCodeCamp.
These were the instructions:
Given a string representing a number, and an integer base from 2 to 36, determine whether the number is valid in that base.

The string may contain integers, and uppercase or lowercase characters.
The check should be case-insensitive.
The base can be any number 2-36.
A number is valid if every character is a valid digit in the given base.
Example of valid digits for bases:
Base 2: 0-1
Base 8: 0-7
Base 10: 0-9
Base 16: 0-9 and A-F
Base 36: 0-9 and A-Z

I imported the string module to define all characters allowed across the different bases. 
To ensure the check is case-insensitive, I converted the input string n to lowercase using .lower().

I then used a for-loop to iterate through each character:
 -First, I verified if the character exists in the set of allowed characters.
 -Then, I determined the index (numeric value) of the character and checked if it is greater than or equal to the base. 
  If so, the function immediately returns False.
If the loop completes without finding an invalid digit, the function returns True.
